course: biology
lesson: 42
title: The capybara: the calmest animal, friend of crocodiles?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The capybara is the biggest rodent in the world, a giant cousin of the guinea pig as heavy as a grown man, that spends its day chewing grass by South American rivers and ponds.
explain: It is famous for staying calm while birds, ducks and even monkeys sit on its back, and for photos of it resting right beside a basking caiman as if they were best friends.
explain: Think of a playground where a big kid sits on the bench next to the school bully: they are not friends, the bully is just not after him right now, and he keeps one eye open all the time.
explain: Caimans, cousins of alligators, sometimes do catch capybaras, mostly young ones, so the truth is calm, not friendship: a big capybara is a hard catch, a sunbathing caiman is not very hungry, and the capybara is ready to run.
explain: In memes and cartoons the capybara is everyone's chilled best friend, even the crocodile's; real capybaras are relaxed and tolerant, but they are still prey and live by watching and listening.
fact: An adult capybara is the largest living rodent, up to about 60 kg, which is too big for most caimans; caimans mostly eat fish, snails, crabs and small animals.
fact: The famous photos are almost always of caimans, not true crocodiles, because capybaras live only in Central and South America, in wetlands such as the Pantanal.
fact: Caimans, especially the big black caiman, sometimes catch and eat capybaras, mostly young ones near the water; jaguars, anacondas and pumas hunt them too.
fact: Capybaras live in groups, often of about 10 to 20; some keep watch, and at a sharp bark-like alarm call the whole group rushes into the water, where they can stay under for about 5 minutes.
fact: Their eyes, ears and nostrils sit high on the head, like a hippo's, so a capybara can hide almost fully under water and still see, hear and breathe; it swims well with webbed toes.
fact: Birds such as cattle tyrants and yellow-headed caracaras ride on capybaras and eat ticks and flies; both gain, which scientists call mutualism, and the capybara may even lie still or roll over for them.
fact: In zoos capybaras let many animals sit on them, but zoo animals are well fed; some viral videos are staged, and in the wild a capybara can bite hard with its big front teeth.
fact: A capybara eats several kilograms of grass and water plants a day, because grass is tough and has little goodness in it; like all rodents its front teeth keep growing all its life and are worn down by chewing.
fact: To get every bit of goodness, capybaras eat some of their own soft droppings in the morning, so the food passes through the gut twice, as rabbits do.
fact: At Izu Shaboten Zoo in Japan, keepers have given capybaras warm baths in winter since the 1980s, and yuzu fruits are often floated in the water; capybaras come from warm places and love water.
fact: Its name is sometimes said to mean «water pig», but the capybara is no pig: it is a close cousin of the guinea pig, both South American rodents, but a capybara can be about 50 times heavier.
fact: Caimans and crocodiles bask with their mouths open to let heat escape and cool down; it is not a threat or a yawn.
fact: A reptile does not burn food to keep warm, so a well-fed caiman may go days or even weeks without a big meal.
fact: Capybaras can sprint at about 35 km/h; in some Argentine towns they now graze on lawns and golf courses, where there is grass, water and no predators, so their numbers grow fast and they can eat gardens bare.
exercise: What kind of animal is a capybara? | The largest rodent in the world | A small kind of wild pig from South America | A big kind of beaver from Africa | A young hippo that has not grown yet
exercise: Why does a basking caiman usually leave a big adult capybara alone? | An adult is too big to tackle, and a basking reptile needs food rarely | Caimans only eat water plants and never touch meat of any kind at all | Capybaras are poisonous to caimans and make them very sick | The capybara has made a deal never to bother the caiman
exercise: Do caimans ever eat capybaras? | Yes, sometimes, mostly young ones near the water | Never, the two of them are lifelong friends who share the river | Only in zoos, where the caimans get bored | Only when the capybara is asleep up in a tree
exercise: What does a capybara family do when one gives a sharp alarm bark? | They all rush into the water | They all freeze and play dead | They all climb the nearest tree | They all turn and fight the enemy
exercise: Why are a capybara's eyes, ears and nose high on its head? | So it can hide in water and still see, hear and breathe | So it can see over the tall grass while lying down to rest | So birds cannot reach them | So its head is lighter for running
exercise: What does the little bird on a capybara's back get, and what does the capybara get? | The bird gets ticks to eat, the capybara gets cleaned | The bird gets a ride home, and the capybara gets nothing back | The bird gets warm, the capybara gets a lookout | Neither gets anything, they just like each other
exercise: What do scientists call a partnership in which both animals gain? | Mutualism | Camouflage | Copycat mimicry | Hibernation
exercise: Why do animals in a zoo video sit so calmly on a capybara? | All are well fed, and the capybara is a calm grass-eater with a broad back | Capybaras give off a special smell that makes other animals feel sleepy | The other animals are trying to eat it slowly, one small bite at a time | Zoo animals have forgotten how to be afraid of anything
exercise: Why does a capybara chew grass all morning? | Grass is tough and low in goodness, so it must eat a lot | It is storing grass in its cheeks for the cold winter | It is only cleaning its long front teeth, not really eating | It only eats in the morning and sleeps the rest of the day
exercise: Why does a capybara eat some of its own soft droppings? | So the food goes through its gut twice and gives more goodness | Because it cannot find enough fresh grass to eat in the dry season | To clean its den | To make itself smell bad to the caimans
exercise: Why do keepers at a Japanese zoo give capybaras hot baths in winter? | They come from warm places and love water, so a warm bath keeps them comfortable | Hot water kills all the fleas on their fur and stops them scratching | The baths help them grow much bigger than their cousins the guinea pigs | It is the only way to make them eat fruit
exercise: Which pet is the capybara's close cousin? | The guinea pig | The pink farm pig | The tame ferret | The pet house rabbit
exercise: Why does a caiman lie in the sun with its mouth wide open? | To let heat escape and cool down | To warn capybaras to keep away | To catch flies on its tongue | Because it is about to yawn
exercise: Why can a caiman go for days without eating? | As a reptile it does not burn food to keep warm | It eats a whole capybara every single day | It drinks lots of muddy river water instead, which fills it up | It sleeps all the time and never moves
exercise: Why have capybaras moved onto golf courses in some Argentine towns? | There is short grass, water and no predators | Golfers feed them bread and chips after every game | They are chasing the golf balls | They were all escaped pets
exercise: What is the truth behind the photos of capybaras and crocodiles as friends? | They are calm neighbours, not friends: caimans sometimes eat capybaras | They really are best friends who protect each other from jaguars | The caiman is the capybara's mother | The photos are all made up with a computer and never really happened
test: «capybaras are friends with literally everything, even crocs, it's on TikTok» What is right? | They are calm and tolerant, but caimans and jaguars still hunt them | Yes, no animal anywhere ever harms a capybara, they're too chill | Yes, crocodiles guard capybaras from jaguars, it's a proper team-up | No, capybaras hunt crocodiles
test: In a cartoon, a crocodile and a capybara share a picnic and the croc never gets hungry. What would a real caiman do? | Ignore an adult while it is full, but sometimes grab a young one | Eat grass and leaves on the bank together with the capybara | Always attack every capybara it sees | Carry baby capybaras in its mouth to keep them safe from jaguars
test: «why doesn't the bloody croc just eat it, it's right there» What is the best answer? | The adult is big, the croc is not that hungry, and it would dash into the water | Caimans cannot open their mouths wide enough for anything bigger than a fish, mate | Capybaras taste of soap | Crocodiles are not allowed to eat rodents, it's against their own rules
test: What would happen to capybaras in a park if jaguars and caimans all disappeared? | Their numbers would grow, and they could eat gardens bare | They would slowly die out without their old friends to keep them company | They would turn into hunters | Nothing at all would change
test: «mate wants to pet a wild capybara, they're basically giant plushies» What is the best advice? | Do not: a wild capybara can bite hard with its big front teeth | Fine, they never bite anyone, they're the chillest animals going | Fine, as long as you bring a duck to sit on it | Only pet it if a caiman is lying nearby
test: «my sis says it's a water pig so it's a pig, done» What is right? | Its name means water pig, but it is a rodent, a guinea pig's cousin | Right, it is a kind of wild pig that just likes swimming in rivers | Right, pigs and capybaras are the same animal | Wrong, it is a kind of small hippo that never grew up properly, innit
test: A yellow bird is pecking at a capybara's back. Is the capybara in trouble? | No, the bird is eating ticks and flies, which helps it | Yes, the bird is eating its fur | Yes, the bird is calling out to the caimans to show them where it is | No, the bird is just keeping warm
test: In a game, your capybara sits in the river with its whole head out of the water to hide. What would a real one do? | Sink so only its eyes, ears and nostrils show | Climb a tree instead | Stay on the bank and growl | Dig a hole in the mud and stay there for hours
test: «no way a capybara's quicker than me lol» What is true? | Over a short burst it can run about 35 km/h, like a fast sprinter | It can only waddle along at about 2 km/h, slower than you walk | It is faster than a cheetah over any distance you like | It cannot run at all, it can only swim and float
test: «guinea pig + capybara both rodents, so same size near enough» What is right? | No, a capybara can be about 50 times heavier | Yes, they weigh about the same, give or take a bit | No, the guinea pig is bigger | Yes, but the capybara has much longer legs, that's all
test: «that croc's yawning at the capybara, it's being well rude» What is really going on? | It is cooling off by letting heat out of its open mouth | It is threatening the capybara | It is tired after a long night of hunting fish in the dark river | It is calling its babies over
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between capybaras and caimans? | The two frequently rest in proximity, yet caimans on occasion prey upon capybaras, chiefly the young | The two species maintain a lasting friendship of mutual defence against the jaguar and the anaconda | Caimans subsist principally upon capybaras and consume them daily | Capybaras habitually prey upon the eggs and young of caimans
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a capybara may be observed consuming its own soft droppings. | The food passes through the gut a second time, permitting the extraction of further nourishment | The animal is thereby rendered unpalatable to the caiman, the jaguar and other predators of the region | The practice serves solely to clean the burrow | The animal is deprived of any other source of food
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the birds which stand upon capybaras. | The birds remove ticks and flies, an arrangement of mutual benefit termed mutualism | The birds feed upon the capybara's fur to its detriment, an arrangement termed parasitism | The birds alert caimans to the location of the capybara | The birds are carried to their nests in exchange for nothing
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an adult caiman may abstain from feeding for many days. | Being a reptile, it does not expend food in maintaining its body heat | It obtains all its nourishment from the water it swallows | It is incapable of digesting any food larger than a snail | It sustains itself by consuming grass in the manner of the capybara upon the bank
